%N A399145 Number of distinct proper side-crossing points among regular polygons inscribed in a common circle and sharing a common vertex.
%C A399145 For each integer n >= 3, let P_n be the regular n-gon inscribed in the same circle, with all polygons sharing one fixed vertex. Let a(n) be the number of distinct proper crossing points among the boundaries of P_3, P_4, ..., P_n. A proper crossing is an intersection lying in the relative interiors of the intersecting sides. Intersections at polygon vertices, including the common vertex and any other coincident vertices on the circumcircle, are not counted.
%C A399145 Conjecture: No three or more sides belonging to distinct polygons P_3, P_4, ..., P_n meet at the same proper crossing point. Hence the number of distinct proper crossing points equals the number of pairwise proper side-crossing events. Verified numerically for n up to 1000.
%H A399145 Eddie Lin Rui, <a href="/A399145/b399145.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 3..1000</a>
%H A399145 Eddie Lin Rui, <a href="/A399145/a399145.png">Illustration for n = 4</a>
%H A399145 Eddie Lin Rui, <a href="/A399145/a399145_1.png">Illustration for n = 5</a>
%H A399145 Eddie Lin Rui, <a href="/A399145/a399145_2.py.txt">Python program for direct numerical verification</a>
%F A399145 Conjecture: a(n) = 2*Sum_{3 <= m < k <= n} (m - GCD(m,k)).
%e A399145 For n = 3, only the triangle (P_3) is present, so a(3) = 0.
%e A399145 For n = 4, the triangle (P_3) and square (P_4) have 4 proper crossings, so a(4) = 4 (see illustration in Links).
%e A399145 For n = 5, the triangle (P_3), square (P_4), and pentagon (P_5) have 14 proper crossings, so a(5) = 14 (see illustration in Links).
%Y A399145 Cf. A006579, A018804.
